<Project>

  <!--
    ============================================================================
    Single-sourced values for the Simitone projects
    ============================================================================
    Added by the build audit (docs/AUDIT_BUILD_DEPS.md, findings C-06, A-01, A-02 and
    the H-08 "no Directory.Build.props anywhere" note).

    What this file is for
    =====================
    Five numbers were written down more than once, and a TFM bump had to be applied in
    three unrelated places, one of them a PowerShell script:

      * net10.0            - Simitone.Client.csproj, Simitone.Tests.csproj,
                             Simitone.GenerateMeshes.csproj, Simitone.PackageRemeshes.csproj,
                             plus PackageRemeshAddon's hardcoded path into
                             Simitone.PackageRemeshes\bin\$(Configuration)\net10.0
      * net10.0-windows    - Simitone.Windows.csproj
      * 14.0 (LangVersion) - Simitone.Tests.csproj, which documented why it set it
                             ("matches the rest of the repo" - the rest being the
                             net10.0 default)
      * SixLabors.ImageSharp 3.1.12 - declared identically in Simitone.Client.csproj and
                             Simitone.Windows.csproj, on purpose, so the two cannot
                             drift
      * MonoGame 3.8.5.1   - the version the whole graph actually resolves to, against
                             3.8.4 in fifteen FreeSO project files

    generate-meshes.ps1 had the two TFMs as string literals, so a bump broke the
    mesh-authoring tool with a "Generator not found" error pointing at the wrong file.
    It now reads them from here through MSBuild, so there is one place to change.

    Scope
    =====
    Client/Simitone only, not the repository root. The FreeSO subtree targets net9.0
    deliberately (it is upstream code tracking upstream), and a root props file would
    invite someone to "fix" that. For the AOT/trim properties that *do* need to reach
    FreeSO, see ../../Directory.Build.targets.

    Nothing here is a Central Package Management file. ManagePackageVersionsCentrally
    would be the real answer for the duplicate PackageReference versions, but it
    requires every project in the graph to drop its Version attribute, and the ones that
    matter (System.Resources.Extensions at three versions, MonoGame at two) are in the
    vendored submodule. Doing that here would either not reach them or fork them. A
    named property per duplicated value gets the drift protection for the files this
    repository owns, without pretending to solve the whole graph.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <SimitoneTargetFramework>net10.0</SimitoneTargetFramework>
    <SimitoneWindowsTargetFramework>net10.0-windows</SimitoneWindowsTargetFramework>
    <SimitoneLangVersion>14.0</SimitoneLangVersion>

    <!-- Declared in both Simitone.Client and Simitone.Windows on purpose: Simitone.Client
         defines the decoder and Simitone.Windows registers it with FreeSO, so both must
         agree on the version rather than trusting a transitive resolution. The property
         is what makes "both declare the same thing" enforceable instead of aspirational. -->
    <SimitoneImageSharpVersion>3.1.12</SimitoneImageSharpVersion>

    <!-- The version the whole restore graph unifies to. Simitone.Windows declares this
         and CopyMonoGameDLLs asserts that what restore resolved matches it, so a
         FreeSO bump to a different MonoGame now fails the build with both version
         numbers in the message instead of quietly changing the renderer. -->
    <SimitoneMonoGameVersion>3.8.5.1</SimitoneMonoGameVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ============================================================================
    BuildIde - the B-12 split between the game build and the -ide build
    ============================================================================
    Added by the build audit (docs/AUDIT_BUILD_DEPS.md, finding B-12).

    THE SITUATION
    =============
    Simitone.Windows references FreeSO's FSO.IDE (the "Volcanic" IDE host) and
    FSO.Client (the TSO online client). FSO.Client ProjectReferences FSO.Server,
    FSO.Server.Database, FSO.Server.DataService, FSO.Server.Clients,
    FSO.Server.Protocol, FSO.Server.Updater, FSO.Server.Common and FSO.Content.TSO -
    which drags in the whole The Sims Online *server*, its ASP.NET Core API host, the
    server watchdog, and MySQL / SQLite / SqlClient / AWS / Octokit / RestSharp /
    Portable.JWT / BouncyCastle on top. FSO.IDE adds FSO.Windows and
    FSO.SimAntics.JIT.Roslyn, and FSO.Client adds VoronoiLib, TargaImage and
    SimplePaletteQuantizer: nineteen projects in all, nine of which Simitone.sln used
    to list.

    None of it survives to the game. Simitone.Windows.csproj's TrimSingleplayerOutput
    target deletes server.dll, watchdog.*, FSO.Server.*, FSO.Common.DataService,
    MySql.Data, System.Data.SqlClient, System.Data.SQLite, AWSSDK.*, Octokit,
    RestSharp, JWT, crypto, NLog and the SSPI/GSSAPI/SQLite natives from $(OutDir)
    immediately after the build. The audit measured 203 of 574 unique warnings (35 %)
    and most of the 10m39s build in those 19 projects.

    The split exists, but the DEFAULT IS NOW BuildIde=true again (see the REVERTED note
    on the property below): a plain

        dotnet build Client/Simitone/Simitone.sln -c Release

    builds the full closure, FSO.Content.TSO's content included. The fast game-only
    build is opt-in:

        dotnet build Client/Simitone/Simitone.sln -c Release -p:BuildIde=false

    which drops the FSO.IDE / FSO.Client references and their closure. A Debug build
    wants the full closure too - see TrimSingleplayerOutput, which is off outside
    Release precisely so the IDE host can be stepped into.

    WHY A PROPERTY AND NOT A SOLUTION FILTER
    =======================================
    A .slnf would have been the smaller change and it does not work here. Building a
    solution filter still follows ProjectReference edges out of the projects the filter
    names, and Simitone.Windows -> FSO.IDE is exactly such an edge, so the filter would
    drag the whole server closure back in and save nothing. Dropping the reference
    requires the reference to be conditional, which requires the property. The solution
    itself was pruned to the game closure as well - see Simitone.sln - because a .sln
    builds every project in its project table whether or not anything references it.

    THE ONE THING THAT STOPS THIS BEING A PURE PROPERTY
    ====================================================
    Program.cs line 268:

        if (ide) new FSO.IDE.VolcanicStartProxy().InitVolcanic(args);

    is the only statement in Simitone.Windows that names FSO.IDE or FSO.Client (the two
    `using FSO.Client;` lines are unused). A one-line call is the whole of the -ide
    feature, and it is not behind a preprocessor symbol, so the reference cannot simply
    be switched off without the file not compiling.

    Rather than leave BuildIde=false unimplementable, Simitone.Windows compiles
    BuildShims/VolcanicStartProxy.NoIde.cs in its place - a type with the same name and
    the same one method, whose InitVolcanic throws PlatformNotSupportedException saying
    this build was made without the IDE host and naming the flag that restores it. That
    turns the failure mode from "the flag silently does nothing" into "the flag tells you
    exactly why, on the only command line that can reach it".

    The shim is checked in rather than generated so that it is reviewable C#, and it is
    excluded from the Compile list by default so that BuildIde=true and BuildIde=false
    can never both be true.

    The residual risk - that something in the shipped closure needed FSO.Client.dll at
    runtime for a reason no reference records - is closed at build time rather than
    taken on faith: _SimitoneAssertNoIdeReferences in Simitone.Windows.csproj reads the
    AssemblyRef/TypeRef names out of every assembly in $(OutDir) and fails the build if
    anything still points at FSO.Client or FSO.IDE. The same reasoning, and the same
    "verify the invariant, do not trust the list" shape, as the trim target.

    SCOPE
    =====
    Client/Simitone only, and in practice only Simitone.Windows.csproj reads it. The
    FreeSO subtree is upstream and is left exactly as it is; nothing here requires a
    FreeSO edit, which is deliberate, so that a submodule bump has nothing to conflict
    with.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      REVERTED to true (2026-09-29). The game build was defaulting to BuildIde=false, which drops
      the whole TSO closure - FSO.IDE, FSO.Client, FSO.Content.TSO and the seventeen projects
      behind them, and with FSO.Content.TSO its 284 MB / 1,405 files of Content. That content drop
      turned out to cost gameplay: the player reported cooked food losing its interactions and
      object-on-object placement (coffeemaker on dishwasher) failing. The instruction is to keep
      every TSO element, so the default is the full closure again. The fast game-only build is
      still available explicitly with -p:BuildIde=false for anyone who wants it, but it is no
      longer what a plain build produces.
    -->
    <BuildIde Condition=" '$(BuildIde)' == '' ">true</BuildIde>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ============================================================================
    Restore-time gates (B-02)
    ============================================================================
    Four NuGet diagnostics promoted from warning to error, for the four Simitone-owned
    projects only. Every one of them is at zero today, which is the whole reason this
    is safe: an error on a diagnostic that already fires is not a gate, it is a build
    that cannot pass, and the audit's lesson from B-01 is that a build which fails for
    a reason nobody chose gets worked around rather than fixed.

      NU1902  a package has a known high-severity vulnerability
      NU1903  a package has a known moderate-severity vulnerability
      NU1904  a package has a known low-severity vulnerability
      NU1702  a package had to fall back to a LOWER target framework than the project
              asks for

    NU1902/1903/1904 are the cheapest real supply-chain control this repository can
    have: a package that acquires an advisory fails the build instead of printing a
    line somebody has to notice. It matters more than usual here, because the audit
    found genuinely abandoned supply chain already in the graph - Portable.JWT 1.0.5
    and Portable.BouncyCastle 1.8.0 are 2016 personal forks, AWSSDK.* is the
    out-of-support v3 line, Octokit is a pre-1.0 package last published in 2019, and
    System.Data.SQLite.Core 1.0.119 was last published in 2018 (A-05, A-07, A-10).
    Nine of those live in the TSO server closure that B-12 took out of the game build,
    which is a second reason the split was worth making: it is a supply-chain
    reduction as well as a build-time one.

    NU1605 is already an error by default and is not repeated here. NU1701 is
    deliberately absent: the packages that produce it - Mina, xxHashSharp,
    FreeSO.HandEvaluator, Common.Logging - are real runtime dependencies with no
    maintained replacement this repository can switch to, so an error would be
    permanent and unfixable rather than a gate. It is censused in the CI run summary
    instead, so it can be watched going down.

    WHY ONLY THE SIMITONE PROJECTS
    ==============================
    Directory.Build.props is not imported by the FreeSO subtree, and that is the
    point. The -ide closure still restores System.Data.SqlClient 4.9.0,
    System.Data.SQLite.Core 1.0.119, AWSSDK.* 3.3.x and Octokit 0.36.0, and at least
    one of those is old enough that NU1903 is a real possibility. Promoting the
    advisories to errors across the whole graph would therefore turn the
    -p:BuildIde=true build into something that fails for reasons nobody in this
    repository can fix - which is how a gate becomes noise. Scope the gate to the
    code this repository owns, and let upstream's own supply chain be upstream's
    problem; that is the same line the B-12 split draws.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1702;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ImageSharp advisories, suppressed one by one (2026-11-14 audit).
    3.2.0 is the first patched 3.x release, but it refuses to build without a Six Labors
    license key, so 3.1.12 (the last release that builds without one) is pinned until a
    license is in place or the dependency is replaced. The advisories are TIFF CCITT
    encode/decode, BigTIFF IFD parsing, ICC CLUT parsing and HistogramEqualization.
    Simitone never encodes TIFF or equalises histograms, and BitmapDecoder only accepts
    BMP and PNG, so the TIFF paths are unreachable from game or mod content. Remove these
    items the moment the version above moves to a patched release: a suppression that
    outlives its reason hides the next advisory of the same id.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-gwg2-r3hj-4w44" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-wmxv-xphr-5c9g" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-j3p4-wp97-rph4" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-j9gm-c75j-xc9q" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-jjfr-hcj7-qf5w" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-v76p-62qx-wwq2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ============================================================================
    Copy retries - the "a running game is holding bin\" half of C-03
    ============================================================================
    Added by the build audit (docs/AUDIT_BUILD_DEPS.md, finding C-03).

    THE PROBLEM
    ===========
    Every target in this repository that touches the output folder is careful
    about a locked file. CopyMonoGameDLLs retries and warns; the trim target
    deletes with ContinueOnError and then names every file that survived, with
    "close the game and rebuild" in the message. The one path that was not
    protected is the SDK's own: Simitone.Client.csproj's content globs are
    CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" items, so they are copied by
    _CopyOutOfDateSourceItemsToOutputDirectory, which calls Copy with the
    SDK's default Retries - that is, zero. Content\MeshReplace alone holds
    5,916 .fsom files, so a build that has to touch any of them goes through
    thousands of copies with no tolerance for a file handle.

    And this is not hypothetical on this machine. The audit's own baseline
    build of the game closure produced MSB3061 "Unable to delete file ...
    testhost.dll ... The file is locked by: testhost (21116)" - the same class
    of failure, on the sibling path, from a test host rather than the game.
    A running game holds far more handles than a test host does.

    WHAT THESE PROPERTIES ARE
    =========================
    CopyRetryCount and CopyRetryDelayMilliseconds are read by the SDK's copy
    targets in Microsoft.Common.CurrentVersion.targets and are passed straight to
    the Copy task's Retries / RetryDelayMilliseconds parameters, which retry a
    failed copy. The MSBuild default is 0 retries: the first failure is the
    final answer. This sets it to the same 4 x 750 ms that CopyMonoGameDLLs
    already uses, so the two paths behave alike.

    WHY IT IS HERE AND NOT ON THE CONTENT ITEMS
    ============================================
    The SDK's copy target does not read retry metadata from the items it copies,
    so there is no per-item spelling of this; the property is the only handle.
    It goes in Directory.Build.props rather than in Simitone.Client.csproj for
    the same reason the other values here do: it is a build-wide property, and
    the 5,916-file case is only the largest instance of the problem - LICENSE,
    NOTICE, Icon.ico and every .png in Content\ go down the same unprotected
    path.

    THE LIMIT, STATED PLAINLY
    =========================
    This narrows the window, it does not close it. Four retries over three
    seconds is enough for a transient handle and not enough for a game that is
    going to keep the file open for as long as it is running. The build still
    fails if the game is open, which is correct - a build that silently did not
    copy half the content would be worse - but it now says so after trying,
    instead of on the first attempt.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <CopyRetryCount>4</CopyRetryCount>
    <CopyRetryDelayMilliseconds>750</CopyRetryDelayMilliseconds>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
